<Base title="How a query runs" description="Trace a PostgreSQL report through scans, joins, grouping and sorting, then test what changes when its input grows or its estimates are wrong.">
  <article class="visual-essay relational-design schema-sketches">
    <header class="essay-header">
      <p class="eyebrow">Databases and storage</p>
      <h1>How a query runs</h1>
      <TitleLine type="Companion" />
    </header>
<ArticleBody>
    <p>A SQL query describes the result you want: which rows to select, how to match them across tables, and what to calculate from them. The database decides how to produce that result. It builds an <strong>execution plan</strong>, a set of operations for reading, joining, grouping and sorting the data.</p>
    <p>There can be several ways to produce the same answer. To join two tables, the database might use an index to look up matching rows one at a time, or read both tables and build a temporary lookup from one of them. A few index searches can save reading a whole table. Thousands of repeated searches may cost more than reading it once. The amount of data each operation handles matters much more than the number of rows in the final result.</p>
    <p>PostgreSQL chooses a plan using estimates of those amounts and the work each operation requires. Its <code>EXPLAIN</code> command lets us inspect that choice, and <code>EXPLAIN ANALYZE</code> runs the query so we can compare the estimates with what happened. This gives us a way to investigate slow queries: we can see where rows accumulate, where work repeats, and where an estimate was wrong. The examples below use PostgreSQL 18 and build on the joins and grouping introduced in <a href="/learn/databases/relational/query-results/">Querying your data</a>.</p>

    <section id="report">
      <h2>The example query</h2>
      <p>Our example is a shop report showing which products a customer bought most. It finds the customer’s orders, joins them to their order lines, and adds up the quantities for each product.</p>
      <p>To run the examples, open a SQL editor or psql connection to a new, empty PostgreSQL 18 practice database. Load <a href={fixture} download="execution-setup.sql">the generated shop setup</a> once, then keep that connection open for the statements below. This is a separate dataset from both the earlier receipt examples and the larger shop used in Indexes. The <a href="/learn/databases/relational/practice/">practice guide</a> explains how to create a practice database.</p>
      <p>The setup creates 10,000 orders shared equally among 1,000 customers. Every order has three lines: one unit of product 1, two of product 2, and three of product 3. Customer 42 owns orders 42, 1042, 2042, and so on through 9042: ten orders in all. With the same quantities on every order, we can work out the totals before running the query.</p>
      <ResultTable caption="The first order belonging to customer 42" columns={['Order ID', 'Line', 'Product ID', 'Quantity']} rows={[[42,1,1,1],[42,2,2,2],[42,3,3,3]]} />
      <p><code>orders</code> has an index on <code>customer_id</code>. The primary key of <code>order_lines</code> creates an index on <code>(order_id, line_no)</code>, which can locate an order’s lines together. The database keeps these indexes up to date as the tables change. <a href="/learn/databases/relational/indexes/">Indexes</a> explains how they work and what they cost to maintain.</p>
      <Sql code={report} label="Count purchased units by product for customer 42" />
      <p>The join returns the 30 lines belonging to customer 42’s ten orders. <code>sum(l.quantity)</code> adds the quantities for each product: ten lots of one, two and three give us totals of 10, 20 and 30. <code>ORDER BY units DESC</code> puts the largest total first, with product ID breaking any tie.</p>
      <ResultTable caption="Customer 42’s report" columns={['product_id','units']} rows={[[3,30],[2,20],[1,10]]} />
      <p>This SQL tells PostgreSQL which rows to match and which totals to calculate. PostgreSQL still has to decide which table to start with and how to find the matching rows.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="narrow-plan">
      <h2>Nested-loop joins</h2>
      <p>Run the report with <code>EXPLAIN ANALYZE</code> in front of it, as shown below. This runs the query and reports what each operation did; plain <code>EXPLAIN</code> would show only the proposed plan. We turn timing off because we’re interested in the row counts here. Bear in mind that <code>EXPLAIN ANALYZE</code> also executes writes when you use it with an <code>INSERT</code>, <code>UPDATE</code> or <code>DELETE</code>.</p>
      <Sql code={'EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, TIMING OFF, BUFFERS OFF, SUMMARY OFF)\n' + report} label="Run the customer report and inspect its execution" />
      <p>Here is the plan from our PostgreSQL 18.3 run, with costs and some other details left out. The operations, indentation and row counts are unchanged. Your installation may choose a different plan.</p>
      <div class="sql-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Observed plan for customer 42"><pre><code>{narrowPlan}</code></pre></div>
      <p>Find the <code>Bitmap Index Scan</code> near the bottom. It searches <code>orders_customer</code> for customer 42 and records where the ten matching rows live. The <code>Bitmap Heap Scan</code> above it uses those locations to fetch the order rows. “Heap” is PostgreSQL’s name for the table storage here.</p>
      <p>The index groups entries by customer, but the orders themselves can be scattered across the table. Collecting their locations in a bitmap lets PostgreSQL visit the table’s pages in physical order, instead of fetching each row as it finds an index entry. Rows fetched this way no longer follow the index’s order. PostgreSQL can also <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/indexes-bitmap-scans.html">combine locations from several indexes</a> in one bitmap, though this query uses only one.</p>
      <p>The <strong>nested loop</strong> takes an order from the first, or <em>outer</em>, branch and passes its ID to the second, or <em>inner</em>, branch. For order 42, the inner index scan finds lines 42/1, 42/2 and 42/3. The join pairs each of those lines with order 42, then repeats the lookup for order 1042, continuing through all ten orders.</p>
      <p>The inner scan reports <code>actual rows=3.00 loops=10</code>: an average of three rows on each of ten executions, giving us 30 lines altogether. All ten executions happen inside this one SQL statement. In other plans, a displayed average may be rounded, so multiplying rows by loops gives an approximate total.</p>
      <p>The join handles only customer 42’s ten orders, leaving out the other 9,990, and the index lookups avoid a scan of all 30,000 lines. It can start returning matches as soon as it finds the first order. Before the report can return a total, though, those matches still have to be grouped and added up.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="grouping">
      <h2>Sorting and aggregation</h2>
      <p>The joined rows arrive one order at a time: product 1, product 2, product 3, then the next order’s products. To total product 1, we need its quantity from all ten orders. This plan first sorts the 30 rows by product ID. All ten product-1 rows become neighbours, followed by the product-2 and product-3 rows.</p>
      <p><code>GroupAggregate</code> can now add up one product’s quantities at a time. It adds ten ones and finishes product 1 when product 2 begins; ten twos make 20, and ten threes make 30. It reads all 30 rows to produce these three totals.</p>
      <p>The report asks for the biggest total first, so there is another <code>Sort</code> above the aggregate. This one orders the three totals by units sold. The earlier sort handled all 30 lines, putting each product’s quantities together so they could be added up; this one has only three rows to arrange.</p>
      <p>PostgreSQL’s <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/executor.html">executor</a> runs the plan by asking each operation for a row; that operation asks those beneath it for the rows it needs. But the <code>Sort</code> cannot supply its first row until it has read and sorted all 30. It is a <strong>blocking operation</strong>: the aggregate has to wait for it to finish. Here, “blocking” refers to waiting for input to be processed, rather than waiting for a lock.</p>
      <p>Adding <code>LIMIT 1</code> would ask for just the highest total. PostgreSQL would still need to read all the matching lines and add their quantities: the product with the largest quantity on one order need not have the largest total across all ten. The limit can reduce work on the final result, but it cannot let this plan stop after reading the first order.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="broad-plan">
      <h2>Hash joins and aggregation</h2>
      <p>Now replace <code>o.customer_id = 42</code> with <code>o.customer_id &lt;= 1000</code>. Every customer in our example qualifies. The totals become 30,000, 20,000 and 10,000 units, still in three result rows. Run the same <code>EXPLAIN</code> command with that changed filter.</p>
      <p>PostgreSQL now chooses a hash join and a hash aggregate. Each builds a hash table, though they use it for different purposes:</p>
      <div class="sql-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Observed plan for all customers"><pre><code>{broadPlan}</code></pre></div>
      <p>Start with the orders branch underneath <code>Hash</code>. A <strong>sequential scan</strong> reads the table and checks the customer condition. All 10,000 orders qualify, so an index would not let this report leave any order out. Reading the table directly avoids looking up every order through the customer index.</p>
      <p>The <code>Hash</code> operation builds a temporary hash table from these orders, using order ID as the key. The other branch scans all 30,000 lines. For each line, PostgreSQL uses a hash of its order ID to find possible matches in the hash table, then compares the IDs to check that they really match. Order 42’s three lines all match the same order row, giving us three joined rows.</p>
      <p>The hash join has to build that table before it can return any matches, and the table takes memory. Once it is built, PostgreSQL can look up each line’s order while scanning the lines. For this report, the planner estimates that two scans and a hash join will cost less than 10,000 separate searches through the lines’ index.</p>
      <p>The <code>HashAggregate</code> builds its own hash table, keyed by <em>product</em> ID. Each joined line updates a running total: a product-2 line adds two to product 2’s entry. There are only three products, so this hash table needs three entries. After reading all 30,000 lines, the aggregate returns the finished totals for the final sort. Keeping a running total for each product saves it from having to sort the lines by product first.</p>
      <PlanFlow />
      <p>Both reports return three rows, but the first joins 30 lines and the second joins 30,000. Looking at those intermediate row counts helps explain why PostgreSQL chose different plans.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="merge">
      <h2>Merge joins</h2>
      <p>The primary-key indexes also let PostgreSQL read both tables in order of order ID. A <strong>merge join</strong> can work through them together. When it reaches order 42, it matches the consecutive lines for 42 before advancing to the next order. Because both inputs are ordered, it can keep moving forwards instead of starting a new search for each line.</p>
      <MergeWalk />
      <p>To see a merge join for this report, we can temporarily discourage the other two join methods. Run the whole block below in the practice connection. The settings apply only within this transaction; <code>ROLLBACK</code> restores them afterwards. This lets us inspect the alternative without changing the connection’s usual planner settings.</p>
      <Sql code={mergeDiagnostic} label="Try the report with a merge join" />
      <p>This produced a <code>Merge Join</code> with an index scan on each primary key, returning the same totals as before. The lines’ index is ordered by <code>(order_id, line_no)</code>, so it can supply rows in the order the join needs. Without suitable indexes, PostgreSQL would have to sort the inputs first. Even with these indexes, its original plan for the report used scanning and hashing.</p>
      <p>The merge join returns rows in order of order ID, which does not help us group by product or rank the totals by units sold. This plan therefore still uses <code>HashAggregate</code> and a final sort.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="estimates">
      <h2>Planner estimates</h2>
      <p>We know customer 42 has ten orders because we generated the data. The planner needs an estimate before it executes the report. PostgreSQL keeps statistics about table size, common values, value distributions and distinct counts. <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/sql-analyze.html"><code>ANALYZE</code></a> refreshes them, using a sample of the rows for large tables.</p>
      <p>Here is the inner index scan from the first plan, this time with its estimates included:</p>
      <div class="sql-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Estimated and actual row counts on a repeated index scan"><pre><code>Index Scan using order_lines_pkey on order_lines l
  (cost=0.29..11.49 rows=3 width=12)
  (actual rows=3.00 loops=10)</code></pre></div>
      <p>PostgreSQL estimated three rows for each index scan, and that is how many it found on average. It also estimated 12 bytes per output row, shown as <code>width=12</code>. The first cost, 0.29, estimates the work needed before the scan can return a row; 11.49 estimates the cost of completing it. These costs use the planner’s own units, rather than milliseconds. Each operation’s cost includes the operations beneath it, so adding all the costs in a plan would count the same work repeatedly.</p>
      <p>A nested loop that looks cheap for ten orders may be expensive for 10,000: the inner scan has to run a thousand times more often. If the planner underestimates the number of orders, it can choose that plan without anticipating the extra lookups. When investigating an unexpectedly slow query, look for the first operation where the estimated and actual row counts differ substantially. Then check how the extra rows affect the operations above it. PostgreSQL’s <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/using-explain.html">Using EXPLAIN</a> guide has more examples of interpreting these counts and costs.</p>
      <p>Our example data also lets us see how an estimate can go wrong. Orders for customers 1–100 have <code>sales_region = 'north'</code>; all others have <code>'south'</code>. Customer 42’s ten orders are therefore all in the north. Run this query with the same <code>EXPLAIN</code> prefix:</p>
      <Sql code={correlated} label="Two related filters on the same orders" />
      <p>Initially, the bitmap index scan estimates ten orders for customer 42 correctly. But the table scan above it estimates only <em>one</em> after applying the north condition; it actually emits ten. Ten per cent of the table’s orders are in the north, and the planner applies that proportion to customer 42’s orders too. It has no statistics telling it that this customer’s orders are <em>all</em> in the north.</p>
      <p>Running <code>ANALYZE orders</code> again would refresh the statistics for each column, but those already describe both distributions correctly. To record the relationship between customer and region, we need to ask for dependency statistics:</p>
      <Sql code={statistics} label="Collect statistics about customer and region together" />
      <p>Run the filtered query’s <code>EXPLAIN</code> again. In our run, the table scan’s estimate rose to ten, matching the ten rows it returned. PostgreSQL still chose a bitmap scan, but now it had an accurate estimate of how many rows would pass both conditions. We changed only its statistics; the orders and indexes stayed the same.</p>
      <p>Dependency statistics can help with equality conditions like these on columns of one table. Their support is more limited for other conditions, and they do not generally fix estimates for joins or correlated expressions. Before adding statistics, check what the planner is missing. If the data has changed since it was last analysed, refreshing the existing statistics may be enough. Here, the missing information was the relationship between two columns. <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/planner-stats.html#PLANNER-STATS-EXTENDED">Extended planner statistics</a> explains the supported cases, and <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/multivariate-statistics-examples.html">the worked examples</a> show other kinds of estimate repair.</p>
      <h3 id="refresh">When statistics are refreshed</h3>
      <p>Statistics are a sample taken at one moment, and each engine has its own rule for taking a new one. Between samples the planner estimates from a table that may no longer exist. <a href="/learn/databases/relational/indexing-queries/#stale">Statistics that no longer match the data</a> shows a join plan going wrong that way.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>PostgreSQL</strong>: autovacuum analyzes a table once the rows changed since the last analysis exceed 50 plus 10% of its rows (<code>autovacuum_analyze_threshold</code> and <code>autovacuum_analyze_scale_factor</code>), on a later pass of its worker, not at that moment. It never analyzes a partitioned table’s parent, only the partitions, so run <code>ANALYZE</code> on the parent yourself after the first load and after large changes. <code>pg_stat_user_tables</code> shows <code>n_mod_since_analyze</code> and when each table was last analyzed.</li>
        <li><strong>InnoDB</strong> (MySQL 8.4) keeps persistent statistics that survive a restart and recalculates them in the background once more than 10% of a table’s rows have changed, sampling 20 index pages by default. Automatic recalculation isn’t replicated; a manual <code>ANALYZE TABLE</code> is, so it runs on replicas too.</li>
        <li><strong>SQL Server</strong> updates a statistic automatically when a query needs it and enough rows have changed since the last update; the threshold shrinks as the table grows.</li>
        <li><strong>SQLite</strong> collects statistics only when asked. Its documentation recommends <code>PRAGMA optimize</code>, which analyzes only the tables that would benefit: run it before closing a short-lived connection, periodically on a long-lived one, and after creating an index.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>A bulk load, restore or large backfill can outrun all of these rules. Finish each one with <code>ANALYZE</code> of the tables it touched (<code>ANALYZE TABLE</code> in MySQL, <code>UPDATE STATISTICS</code> in SQL Server, plain <code>ANALYZE</code> in SQLite, because <code>PRAGMA optimize</code> skips a table that grew less than tenfold). In PostgreSQL, creating an expression index also needs an <code>ANALYZE</code> before the planner has statistics for the expression.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="memory">
      <h2>Memory and temporary files</h2>
      <p>The report across all customers uses memory in several places. The hash join stores 10,000 order IDs, the hash aggregate keeps three running totals, and the final sort orders those three totals. The aggregate needs one entry for each distinct product, however many lines it reads. More orders make the join’s hash table grow; more distinct products make the aggregate’s hash table grow.</p>
      <p>PostgreSQL’s <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/runtime-config-resource.html#GUC-WORK-MEM"><code>work_mem</code> setting</a> provides a base memory limit for operations such as sorts, with a multiplier for hash operations. Each operation can use its own allowance, so several operations in one query, or several queries running together, can use much more than <code>work_mem</code> in total. When a sort runs out of allowed memory, it can sort portions of its input into temporary files and merge them. Hash operations can also split their work into batches that use temporary storage.</p>
      <p>We can make a sort spill to temporary files by giving it a small memory allowance. The query below sorts all 30,000 lines by product and order ID, which takes more memory than sorting the three totals in our report.</p>
      <Sql code={spill} label="Sort all order lines with 64 kB of working memory" />
      <p>Our run reported <code>Sort Method: external merge</code>, <code>Disk: 544kB</code>, and counts of temporary blocks read and written, confirming that the sort used temporary storage. The size may differ on your installation. We ran these examples in PGlite, so this does not measure the speed of a server’s physical disk. <code>ROLLBACK</code> restores the connection’s previous memory setting.</p>
      <p>Before increasing <code>work_mem</code>, check what the query is sorting. If the application needs only product totals, returning every line makes the sort do unnecessary work. If it really needs all the lines in this order, more memory may help, but the setting also affects other operations and concurrent queries.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="reading-a-plan">
      <h2>Reading an execution plan</h2>
      <p class="example-note">The <a href="/learn/databases/relational/sandbox/?tutorial=join-work">join-work lesson</a> runs the narrow and broad reports from this article. Follow the nested loop’s repeated probes, compare a hash join with a merge join, then change how many customers qualify. For a smaller first plan, the <a href="/learn/databases/relational/sandbox/?tutorial=query-plans">execution-plan lesson</a> follows a report from 1,000 sale records to three customer totals. Predict each operation’s output, then compare estimates with actual row counts in PostgreSQL.</p>
      <p>When you read a plan for your own query, start with the scans near the bottom. Check which rows they read and which they filter out, then follow those rows through the joins. A repeated index scan may look small until you multiply its average row count by <code>loops</code>. Compare estimates with actual counts at each operation, and look for sorts and aggregates that have to finish reading their input before they can return a row.</p>
      <p>The plan gives you specific things to investigate: a filter that passes many more rows than expected, an index lookup repeated thousands of times, or a sort that spills to temporary files. Change one thing, run the query again, and compare both the plan and the result to check that the answer is still correct.</p>
      <p><a href={experiments} download="execution-experiments.sql">The complete experiment script</a> runs after the setup and includes all three join methods, the statistics comparison, and the sort spill. Start again in a new, empty practice database before repeating the statistics experiment so that it starts without the dependency statistics we added.</p>
    </section>
    <section class="essay-sources" id="further-reading">
      <h2>Sources and further reading</h2>
      <p>Researched and checked 15 October 2026. This generated shop is an invented teaching example. Query results, plan operations, statistics changes and the sort spill were executed with PostgreSQL 18.3 through PGlite 0.5.8. The narrow and broad plans used the default planner settings; the merge example deliberately discourages the competing joins. These observations are not a server performance benchmark.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/using-explain.html">Using EXPLAIN</a>: a longer worked introduction to scans, joins, estimates and observed execution.</li>
        <li><a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/planner-optimizer.html">Planner and optimizer</a>: how PostgreSQL builds candidate paths for joins and other work.</li>
        <li><a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/executor.html">Executor</a>: how operations request and supply rows through a plan tree.</li>
        <li><a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/row-estimation-examples.html">Row estimation examples</a>: how value frequencies and distributions become a predicted number of rows.</li>
        <li><a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/sql-explain.html">EXPLAIN reference</a>: the exact meanings of timing, buffer, memory and temporary-storage output.</li>
        <li>When statistics refresh, checked 18 October 2026: PostgreSQL’s <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/routine-vacuuming.html#VACUUM-FOR-STATISTICS">Updating planner statistics</a> and <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/populate.html">Populating a database</a>; MySQL 8.4’s <a href="https://dev.mysql.com/doc/refman/8.4/en/innodb-persistent-stats.html">Persistent optimizer statistics</a>; SQL Server’s <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/relational-databases/statistics/statistics">Statistics</a>; SQLite’s <a href="https://www.sqlite.org/pragma.html#pragma_optimize">PRAGMA optimize</a>.</li>
        <li><a href="/learn/databases/relational/postgresql/query-execution/">Planning and executing a PostgreSQL query</a>: prepared plans, unequal tenant sizes and working memory across concurrent operations.</li>
        <li><a href="/learn/databases/relational/query-results/">Querying your data</a>: joins, grouping and the results they produce.</li>
        <li><a href="/learn/databases/relational/indexes/">Indexes</a>: how index order affects the rows a query has to read.</li>
